exists exactly when \(N\mid p^s\): applying it to \(p^s1=0\) proves necessity; reduction proves sufficiency. Thus “impossible” needs the prime-power exception. It cannot transfer the construction to a general semiprime. E4 fails at the target connection, before any relation-yield calculation; no compatible descent has been supplied.

**KNOWN — \(\Lambda\)-rings** organize simultaneous commuting Frobenius lifts; \(\psi^p(T)=T^p\) on \(\mathbf Z[T]\) is a genuine example. Borger’s \(\mathbf F_1\) interpretation formalizes this structure. [Borger](https://maths-people.anu.edu.au/~borger/papers/_all/LambdaRingsAndTheFieldWithOneElement.pdf)

**PROVED — Why Fermat does not transfer.** Evaluation at \(a\) satisfies
\[
\operatorname{ev}_a(\psi^pT)=a^p,
\]
whereas every unital endomorphism of \(\mathbf Z/N\) fixes \(a\). Equivariance therefore requires the additional condition \(a^p\equiv a\pmod N\).

For squarefree \(N=PQ\), the exact fraction of residues satisfying this condition is
\[
\frac{(1+\gcd(p-1,P-1))(1+\gcd(p-1,Q-1))}{N}
\le\frac{p^2}{N}.
\]
Each field contributes zero plus the roots of unity of order dividing \(p-1\); CRT proves the formula.

**HEURISTIC — Yield.** Under uniform-residue sampling, \(M\) smooth inputs give at most \(Mp^2/N\) expected compatible evaluations. With \(M,p=L[\alpha,O(1)]\), this is \(\exp(-x+o(x))\). Smoothness does not repair the missing equivariance.

**PROVED — The integer splitting error is visible explicitly:**
\[
T^p-T=\prod_{a=0}^{p-1}(T-a)+pC_p(T),
\qquad C_p\in\mathbf Z[T].
\]
Reduction modulo \(p\) proves coefficientwise divisibility. Modulo \(N\), the correction remains; it is not systematically smooth or zero.

**PROVED — Group rings and cyclotomic automorphisms.** For \((a,m)=1\), \(\zeta_m\mapsto\zeta_m^a\) is an automorphism over every \(\mathbf Z/N\). It permutes conjugates, but does not act as a power map on arbitrary sums. Norm identities multiply the conjugates together and incur the corresponding degree/height cost. Thus E1–E3 have formal analogues; E4/E5 remain missing.

Auxiliary idempotents need not factor \(N\): in a group ring with \(g^2=1\) and \(2\) invertible, \((1+g)/2\) is idempotent for every \(N\). It splits the known auxiliary algebra.

**PROVED — Three precise barriers, with limited scope.**

1. **Small integer products.** If both positive sides have at most \(r\) factors of logarithmic size at most \(H\), and \(rH<x\), a congruence between them is an integer equality. The number of nontrivial such congruences is exactly zero. Factor-base valuation rows from exact integer equalities are zero.

2. **Polynomial representations.** If coprime \(f,g\in\mathbf Z[T]\) share a root modulo \(N\), then
   \[
   0<N\le|\operatorname{Res}(f,g)|
   \le\|f\|_2^{\deg g}\|g\|_2^{\deg f}.
   \]
   The integer Bézout identity for the resultant gives divisibility by \(N\); Hadamard applied to the Sylvester matrix gives the upper bound. If the resultant is zero, this argument gives no bound.

3. **Base-ring endomorphisms.** A unital endomorphism fixes \(1\), hence every integer residue, so it is the identity. A nonunital endomorphism has form \(a\mapsto ae\), with \(e^2=e\). A nontrivial such \(e\) already yields a proper factor \(\gcd(e,N)\).

**PROVED — A correction to the proposed example.** Substitution \(T\mapsto T^N\) is always an endomorphism of \((\mathbf Z/N)[T]\). It descends through \(F\) exactly when \(F(T^N)=0\) modulo \(F\). This differs from mapping every element \(u\mapsto u^N\). Even the latter can work for composites: on \(\mathbf Z/561\), it is the identity, by CRT and Fermat since \(2,10,16\mid560\).

**HEURISTIC — Scope of the wall.** “Every smooth construction is a product of small linear forms” is not a useful universal theorem: writing its prime factorization merely restates smoothness. Nor must every useful congruence arise from two fixed coprime univariate polynomials. The resultant theorem supports the stated polynomial-selection ledger; it does not establish a universal classical \(1/3\) barrier.

**SPECULATIVE — Best remaining mechanism.** Auxiliary algebras are still worth considering because they admit genuine automorphisms. A credible proposal must add an \(N\)-dependent evaluation and an elimination that reduces both algebraic complexity and logarithmic height, with controlled branching and exceptional cases. None of the listed identities supplies that step. I would prioritize finding that operation over collecting further splitting identities.

**HEURISTIC — A cheap kill test for the strongest actual smooth-number supply.** Use
\[
N=1{,}000{,}003\cdot1{,}000{,}033
 =1{,}000{,}036{,}000{,}099
\]
and \(B=4096\). Enumerate \(\binom nk\) for \(n\le4096\), \(2\le k\le n/2\), retaining values of at least 80 bits. Remove exact duplicate integers, then count distinct-value collisions modulo \(N\). This tests whether constructed smooth values have an excess of modular identifications.

**PROVED — Null prediction.** For \(M\) independent uniform units, the expected collision count is exactly
\[
\frac{M(M-1)}{2\varphi(N)}.
\]
The experiment has \(M=4{,}155{,}871\), giving **8.6353362044 expected collisions**. Uniform-residue behavior of the binomial family is the heuristic being tested.

**PROVED — Computed outcome.** The exact run found **10 collisions**, with parity-row rank **10**, against a factor base of **564 primes**. Independently sampled random integers of matching bit lengths, conditioned on being units, gave **8 collisions**. Runtime was about 37 seconds. The [script](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/systeq_1/systeq_blind_binomial_test.py) and [results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/systeq_1/systeq_blind_binomial_result.json) are reproducible.

**HEURISTIC — Interpretation.** This shows no excess modular yield. It grants much larger values than the desired asymptotic budget, so it is deliberately generous. It rejects this direct collision strategy as promising evidence; it does not exclude an adaptive descent with additional arithmetic structure.
